const STRUCTURAL_HEALTH_CONTENT = {
  "joint_comfort_collagen": "Joint comfort depends on how well your cartilage cushions movement, how smoothly your joint surfaces glide, and how stable the surrounding tissues feel. Collagen is the structural material that supports all of this. It forms the framework of cartilage, strengthens ligaments and tendons, and helps maintain the soft, hydrated environment that keeps joints moving without stiffness or discomfort.\nWhen collagen levels are healthy, joints feel flexible, cushioned, and supported. When collagen breaks down, joints can feel tight, noisy, or sore — especially during movement. Collagen supports joint comfort by activating cartilage building cells ((chondrocyte_activation)), switching on natural repair pathways ((matrix_repair_signalling)), and helping calm inflammation inside the joint ((inflammation_resolution_dynamics)).\n\n### 🧱 Chondrocyte Activation\n\n- Collagen helps stimulate the cells that rebuild cartilage, increasing the production of structural proteins that keep joint surfaces smooth ((cartilage_matrix_production)).\n- It supports the formation of collagen type II and proteoglycans, the key molecules that give cartilage its bounce and shock absorbing ability ((collagen_type_II_and_proteoglycans)).\n- These pathways help repair worn or rough cartilage areas, reducing friction during movement ((damaged_cartilage_surfaces)).\n- As cartilage becomes healthier, joints gain better cushioning and resilience, improving comfort during daily activity ((joint_cushioning_and_resilience)).\n\n### 🔗 Matrix Repair Pathways\n\n- Collagen helps activate the body’s natural rebuilding signals, allowing joint tissues to repair themselves more effectively ((matrix_repair_signalling)).\n- It supports TGF β driven regeneration, which maintains the structure and turnover of the extracellular matrix ((ECM_rebuilding_and_turnover)).\n- Collagen pathways enhance the stability of aggrecan and hyaluronan, molecules that keep cartilage hydrated and springy ((synthesis_and_stability)).\n- Stronger collagen fibres help spread pressure evenly across the joint, reducing stress on sensitive areas ((load_distribution)).\n- This leads to balanced, healthy remodelling, preventing excessive breakdown over time ((balanced_matrix_remodelling)).\n\n### ➿ Inflammation Resolution\n\n- Collagen helps reduce inflammatory signals that accelerate cartilage wear and joint discomfort ((cartilage_breakdown)).\n- It encourages a shift toward pro repair immune activity, helping the joint move from damage to healing ((pro_repair_M2_state)).\n- These pathways help lower oxidative stress, protecting cartilage cells from further damage ((tissue_protection)).\n- Together, they create a joint environment that supports regeneration, not breakdown ((regeneration_and_healing)).\n\n### ✨ Functional Joint Outcomes\n\n- Joints feel more mobile and easier to move, thanks to smoother cartilage and better lubrication ((mobility_and_range_of_motion)).\n- Daily stiffness and discomfort decrease as friction and inflammation reduce ((stiffness_and_discomfort)).\n- Cartilage becomes stronger and better hydrated, helping maintain its thickness and long term integrity ((cartilage_integrity_and_thickness)).\n- Collagen strengthens the surrounding tissues, improving overall joint stability and functional support ((joint_stability_and_function)).\n\n### 🟢 Outcome\n\nCollagen is essential for joint comfort. It activates the cells that rebuild cartilage, supports the body’s natural repair pathways, and helps calm inflammation inside the joint. Over time, this leads to smoother movement, less stiffness, stronger cushioning, and more resilient joints.",
  "collagen_mineral_scaffold": "Bone strength is not defined by hardness alone. True skeletal strength comes from a dual layer design:\na flexible collagen framework on the inside and a dense mineral layer on the outside. Collagen is the part that gives bones their resilience — the ability to bend slightly, absorb impact, and resist cracking. Minerals then harden this framework, creating the rigid structure we recognise as bone.\nWhen collagen is healthy, bones are strong, stable, and capable of handling daily stress. When collagen declines, bones lose their internal flexibility and become brittle — even if calcium intake is normal. This is why collagen is the foundation of lifelong bone strength.\n\n### 🧱 Collagen creates the bone’s internal architecture\n\n- Collagen forms a woven, three dimensional network that shapes the internal structure of bone and gives it controlled flexibility ((collagen_scaffold_structure)).\n- This network acts as a template for mineral attachment, guiding where minerals settle and how they organise ((mineral_binding_template)).\n- A strong collagen matrix allows bones to absorb shock and impact, reducing the risk of micro cracks during movement or exercise ((impact_absorption_support)).\n- Collagen maintains the balance between stiffness and flexibility, preventing bones from becoming overly rigid or fragile ((bone_flexibility_balance)).\n- This internal architecture is what allows bones to bend slightly under pressure instead of snapping — a property essential for everyday movement.\n\n### 🔗 Collagen directs mineralisation and hardening\n\n- Collagen provides the initial anchor points where calcium and phosphate begin to crystallise into hydroxyapatite ((mineral_nucleation_guidance)).\n- It ensures minerals are evenly distributed, creating dense, uniform bone tissue rather than weak, patchy areas ((mineral_distribution_uniformity)).\n- Strong collagen fibres allow minerals to penetrate deeply, improving bone density and long term structural strength ((mineral_integration_depth)).\n- The quality of collagen determines how tightly minerals bind, influencing fracture resistance and overall bone durability ((mineral_binding_efficiency)).\n- When collagen quality declines, minerals attach poorly — leading to bones that may appear dense but behave fragile.\n\n### 🦴 Collagen supports bone building and bone monitoring cells\n\n- Collagen helps activate osteoblasts, the cells responsible for forming new bone tissue ((osteoblast_activation_support)).\n- It provides the structural environment osteoblasts need to lay down fresh bone matrix (osteoid) ((osteoid_matrix_formation)).\n- Collagen supports osteocytes — the cells embedded within bone — helping them sense stress, pressure, and micro damage ((osteocyte_signal_support)).\n- It helps maintain the balance between bone formation and bone breakdown, preventing excessive loss of bone mass ((bone_turnover_balance)).\n- This cellular support is essential for maintaining bone strength throughout adulthood and ageing.\n\n### ➿ Collagen maintains bone renewal, repair, and longevity\n\n- Bones constantly remodel themselves, and collagen supports this continuous renewal cycle that keeps bones strong and adaptable ((bone_remodelling_cycle)).\n- It helps repair tiny cracks and micro damage caused by daily stress before they develop into larger fractures ((microdamage_repair_support)).\n- Collagen pathways reduce oxidative stress inside bone tissue, protecting bone building cells from premature ageing ((bone_oxidative_protection)).\n- A strong collagen network improves bone elasticity, allowing bones to withstand sudden forces, twists, or impacts without breaking ((bone_elasticity_support)).\n- Over time, healthy collagen supports long term bone resilience, helping maintain strength well into later life ((bone_resilience_improvement)).\n\n### ✨ Functional bone outcomes\n\n- Bones become stronger, denser, and more resilient, with improved ability to handle everyday stress ((bone_density_support)).\n- Better mineral attachment leads to greater structural stability, reducing the likelihood of fractures ((fracture_risk_reduction)).\n- Healthy collagen supports lifelong bone integrity, helping maintain skeletal strength as part of healthy ageing ((lifelong_bone_integrity)).\n- The combined effect is bones that feel stronger, recover faster, and remain structurally sound over decades.\n\n### 🟢 Outcome\n\nCollagen is the foundation of bone strength. It builds the flexible scaffold that minerals attach to, supports the cells that create new bone, and maintains the repair cycles that keep bones strong throughout life. When collagen is healthy, bones are dense, resilient, and able to withstand impact. When collagen declines, bones lose their flexibility and become more prone to fractures — even if calcium intake is adequate.",
  "nutrient_delivery_network": "Nutrient delivery pathways explain how minerals, peptides, and bioactive proteins travel from digestion → bloodstream → target tissues. These pathways determine how efficiently nutrients reach bones, joints, skin, and connective tissues. Their performance depends on multiple absorption routes ((multi_route_absorption)), specialised transport systems ((carrier_mediated_transport)), and precise tissue level targeting ((tissue_specific_targeting)).\nOvoMatrix and ComboCollagen naturally support these pathways, helping nutrients move where they are needed most.\n\n### 🧱 Gastrointestinal Uptake Routes\n\n- Minerals enter the body through ion based absorption channels ((active_and_passive_ion_transport)).\n- Peptides move across the gut wall using dedicated peptide transport systems ((transporter_mediated_peptide_uptake)).\n- Larger proteins and complexes use cellular engulfing and transfer routes ((endocytic_and_transcytotic_routes)).\n- Micro porous structures improve dissolution and nutrient release, enhancing uptake efficiency ((dissolution_and_release_dynamics)).\n- Together, these routes ensure that different nutrient types can enter circulation through the most efficient pathway available.\n\n### 🔗 Circulatory Transport Mechanisms\n\n- Calcium travels through the bloodstream using protein bound and free ion pools ((albumin_and_free_ion_pools)).\n- Peptides circulate while maintaining stability and bioactivity in plasma ((plasma_transport_and_stability)).\n- Bioactive proteins interact with carrier networks that protect and deliver them to tissues ((carrier_protein_networks)).\n- Nutrient complexes maintain system wide availability, ensuring they remain active until they reach their destination ((systemic_bioactive_availability)).\n- These mechanisms keep nutrients stable, protected, and ready for delivery.\n\n### ➿ Targeted Tissue Delivery\n\n- Bone cells respond to mineral based signalling cues, improving mineral uptake and bone formation ((mineral_signalling_and_uptake)).\n- Chondrocytes use peptide driven repair signals to support cartilage regeneration ((peptide_driven_cartilage_repair)).\n- Fibroblasts activate collagen building pathways, strengthening skin and connective tissues ((collagen_matrix_regeneration)).\n- Joint tissues benefit from coordinated nutrient distribution, supporting lubrication, cushioning, and mobility ((integrated_nutrient_distribution)).\n- This targeted delivery ensures each tissue receives the nutrients it needs most.\n\n### 🌿 Functional Outcomes\n\n- Improved bone density and mineralisation, supporting long term skeletal strength ((bone_density_and_mineralisation)).\n- Enhanced joint mobility and cartilage integrity, reducing stiffness and supporting comfort ((joint_mobility_and_cartilage_integrity)).\n- Strengthened skin structure and elasticity, improving firmness and resilience ((skin_structure_and_elasticity)).\n- Optimised connective tissue performance, supporting tendons, ligaments, and fascia ((connective_tissue_performance)).\n- These outcomes reflect a nutrient system that is efficient, targeted, and biologically active.\n\n### 🟢 Outcome\n\nA coordinated, multi route nutrient delivery system ensures that minerals, peptides, and proteins reach their target tissues efficiently. This supports regeneration, structural integrity, and long term tissue health across the entire body.",
  "collagen_matrix_integrity": "Collagen matrix integrity describes the strength, organisation, and long term resilience of the collagen network that supports skin, joints, bones, tendons, ligaments, and fascia.\nThis matrix is not static — it is a dynamic, living structure that constantly remodels, repairs, and reorganises itself in response to mechanical load, hydration, ageing, and nutrient availability.\nIts performance depends on three core pillars:\n- How well collagen fibres align ((fibrillar_alignment_quality))\n- How balanced and mature the crosslinks are ((crosslink_density_balance))\n- How hydrated and pliable the matrix remains ((matrix_hydration_state))\nWhen these elements work together, tissues stay firm, elastic, shock absorbing, and capable of long term regeneration.\n\n### 🧱 Fibrillar Organisation\n\nFibrillar organisation refers to how collagen fibres are arranged, aligned, and structured within a tissue. This determines how well the tissue can handle mechanical stress.\n- Triple helix fibres form the primary tension bearing backbone, giving tissues their load carrying strength and structural stability ((tensile_strength_and_stability)).\n- When fibres are aligned in an orderly pattern, tissues can distribute mechanical forces efficiently, preventing localised stress points that lead to micro tears ((load_distribution_efficiency)).\n- The body regulates fibril diameter to maintain uniformity across the matrix, ensuring that fibres behave consistently under pressure ((matrix_uniformity)).\n- When fibrils are cohesive and well organised, tissues gain resilience, allowing them to stretch, compress, twist, and recover without structural damage ((tissue_resilience)).\nThis organisation is what keeps skin firm, tendons strong, and joints stable under daily mechanical load.\n\n### 🔗 Crosslinking Dynamics\n\nCrosslinking dynamics describe how collagen fibres are chemically bonded to each other, creating a stable yet flexible network.\nThis is one of the most important — and most misunderstood — aspects of collagen science.\n- Enzymatic crosslinks (like lysyl oxidase mediated bonds) create reinforced fibre connections, increasing the matrix’s ability to withstand tension, compression, and repetitive mechanical stress ((mechanical_strength)).\n- When crosslinking is balanced, the matrix remains flexible enough to move yet strong enough to resist deformation. Too few crosslinks → weak tissues. Too many → brittle tissues ((matrix_brittleness)).\n- Mature crosslinks stabilise the collagen network over long periods, supporting decades long tissue integrity rather than short term structural strength ((long_term_tissue_integrity)).\n- Controlled bonding patterns allow fibres to stretch and recoil, giving tissues their natural elasticity and preventing permanent deformation ((elasticity_and_flexibility)).\nCrosslinking is the reason youthful tissues feel springy and resilient, while ageing tissues become stiff, rigid, and prone to tearing.\n\n### ➿ Matrix Repair & Turnover\n\nThe collagen matrix is constantly being broken down and rebuilt — a process essential for tissue longevity.\n- Fibroblasts regulate collagen synthesis and remodelling, adjusting fibre orientation and density based on mechanical demand ((collagen_synthesis_and_remodelling)).\n- Bioactive peptides activate repair pathways, accelerating the restoration of damaged or fragmented collagen fibres ((matrix_repair_pathways)).\n- Enzymes like MMPs and TIMPs maintain healthy turnover, clearing old or dysfunctional fibres and replacing them with new, functional collagen ((healthy_collagen_turnover)).\n- This renewal restores structural continuity, ensuring tissues remain smooth, stable, and capable of long term performance ((structural_continuity)).\nWithout proper turnover, tissues accumulate damage and lose their mechanical integrity.\n\n### ✨ Functional Tissue Outcomes\n\n- Skin maintains firmness and elasticity, resisting sagging, wrinkling, and deformation ((firmness_and_elasticity)).\n- Joints retain cartilage support and stability, improving comfort and reducing wear and tear ((cartilage_support_and_stability)).\n- Bones benefit from a stronger collagen scaffold for mineral attachment, improving density, shock absorption, and fracture resistance ((collagen_scaffold_for_mineralisation)).\n- Connective tissues gain durability and enhanced repair capacity, supporting tendons, ligaments, and fascia under daily mechanical load ((durability_and_repair_capacity)).\n\n### 🟢 Outcome\n\nA strong collagen matrix is the foundation of mechanical strength, elasticity, and regenerative capacity across the entire body.\nWhen matrix integrity is high, tissues stay firm, stable, and resilient.\nWhen it declines, tissues weaken, lose structure, and become more prone to damage, stiffness, and premature ageing."
};

