const ESC_ABSORPTION_CONTENT = {
  "esc_absorption": "Most calcium supplements behave like ground up stone — dense, slow to dissolve, and heavily dependent on stomach acid. ESC is different. Because it comes from a biological structure, your body treats it more like a nutrient than a mineral deposit. This is why CalPure feels smoother, lighter, and more effective: ESC follows the body’s natural absorption logic instead of fighting against it.\n\n### 💧 ESC Begins Dissolving Before Acid Even Arrives\n\nRock calcium (especially carbonate) needs strong stomach acid to break down. If acidity is low — which is common in adults — absorption drops sharply. ESC bypasses this problem entirely.\n- Water enters ESC’s porous lattice instantly ((water_permeation_initiation))\n- The mineral softens even before full acid exposure ((pre_acid_softening_behavior))\n- Dissolution begins at normal stomach pH, not just high acid conditions ((low_acid_dissolution_support))\n- This reduces the digestive strain associated with carbonate tablets ((reduced_acid_dependency))\n\n### 🧬 A Gradual Release That Matches the Body’s Rhythm\n\nThe body absorbs minerals best when they arrive gradually, not in a sudden flood. ESC naturally follows this pattern.\n- Calcium detaches progressively from the mineral–protein matrix ((stepwise_mineral_release))\n- This matches the pace of intestinal calcium transporters ((transporter_friendly_flux))\n- The body receives calcium in small, usable waves ((wave_pattern_absorption))\n- This avoids the “overload then waste” problem of dense mineral salts ((uptake_smoothness_factor))\n\n### 🚚 ESC Moves Through the Gut More Smoothly\n\nBecause ESC dissolves evenly, it interacts more gently with the digestive environment.\n- Less unabsorbed calcium remains in the gut ((low_residual_mineral_load))\n- Lower chance of bloating or heaviness ((digestive_lightness_effect))\n- Reduced risk of constipation compared to carbonate ((reduced_gut_compaction))\n- Better comfort leads to better daily compliance ((daily_comfort_compliance))\n\n### 🔄 ESC Aligns With the Body’s Natural Mineral Transport System\n\nOnce dissolved, ESC continues to behave differently from rock calcium.\n- Its mineral–protein fragments remain more soluble ((matrix_assisted_solubility_support))\n- This helps calcium stay available during intestinal transit ((intestinal_phase_stability))\n- The body can direct the mineral more efficiently to bones and tissues ((biologically_coordinated_utilisation))\n\n### 🟢 Outcome\n\nYour body absorbs ESC better because ESC behaves like a biological mineral, not a geological one. It dissolves earlier, releases gradually, moves smoothly, and aligns with natural nutrient pathways.",
  "esc_bioavailability": "Most calcium sources struggle with one simple problem: the body doesn’t recognise them. They behave like ground stone — dense, inert, and difficult to dissolve. ESC is different because it comes from a biological structure built for mineral delivery, not geological storage. This natural design is the reason CalPure offers a form of calcium that the body can absorb with far less effort.\n\n### 🧬 A Biological Mineral the Body Instantly Understands\n\nESC originates from a living system, so its mineral form is already aligned with human biology.\n- Its structure resembles food based calcium, not rock ((food_like_mineral_behavior))\n- The mineral is bound to proteins and collagen fragments ((mineral_protein_matrix))\n- These biological cues help the body recognise and process ESC more efficiently ((biogenic_calcium_source))\n- The result is a mineral that feels “native” to the digestive system ((biological_uptake_support))\n\n### 💧 A Porous Structure That Makes Calcium More Available\n\nBioavailability begins with dissolution — and ESC dissolves in a way that supports absorption.\n- Its micro porous lattice increases surface area ((surface_area_bioaccessibility_gain))\n- Water penetrates the structure instantly ((water_permeation_initiation))\n- Dissolution begins even at lower stomach acidity ((low_acid_dissolution_support))\n- The mineral softens instead of resisting breakdown ((gentle_dissolution_profile))\n\n### 🚚 A Release Pattern That Matches the Body’s Transport System\n\nThe body absorbs calcium best when it arrives gradually, not in a sudden spike. ESC naturally provides this rhythm.\n- ESC releases calcium in a stepwise pattern ((stepwise_mineral_release))\n- This aligns with intestinal calcium transporters ((transporter_friendly_flux))\n- The body receives calcium in small, usable waves ((wave_pattern_absorption))\n- This reduces the amount of calcium wasted or excreted ((controlled_bioavailability_profile))\n\n### 🧩 Protein Bound Calcium Stays More Soluble\n\nOne of ESC’s biggest advantages is that its mineral–protein matrix keeps calcium from clumping or precipitating.\n- Protein fragments help maintain calcium solubility ((matrix_assisted_solubility_support))\n- This prevents the formation of dense, unusable mineral clusters ((reduced_inert_mineral_behavior))\n- Calcium remains available throughout intestinal transit ((intestinal_phase_stability))\n- More soluble calcium means more calcium absorbed ((bioavailable_calcium_complexes))\n\n### 🟢 Outcome\n\nESC is naturally high bioavailability calcium because it is biological, porous, protein bound, soluble, and released in a controlled, food like pattern. This is why CalPure delivers calcium that feels gentle, absorbs efficiently, and supports the body without the digestive strain of traditional supplements.",
  "esc_nutrient_delivery": "ESC doesn’t just dissolve better — it moves through the body differently. Because it comes from a biological structure, ESC follows the same nutrient delivery logic your body already uses for food minerals. This is one of the reasons CalPure feels efficient: the calcium doesn’t get stuck, clump, or pass through unused. It travels smoothly, stays soluble, and reaches the places where it’s actually needed.\n\n### 🧬 ESC Starts Its Journey as a Mineral–Protein Complex\n\nOnce ESC begins dissolving, it doesn’t break into raw, isolated calcium ions immediately. Instead, it forms small mineral–protein fragments that behave like food derived nutrients.\n- These fragments remain more soluble in the intestinal environment ((matrix_assisted_solubility_support))\n- They avoid forming dense, unusable mineral clusters ((reduced_inert_mineral_behavior))\n- The protein components help guide calcium through digestive fluids ((protein_guided_transport_facilitation))\n- This keeps calcium in a “bioavailable state” for longer ((intestinal_phase_stability))\n\n### 🚚 ESC Moves Smoothly Through the Intestinal Pathways\n\nThe body absorbs minerals through specific transport routes — and ESC aligns naturally with them.\n- Gradual release matches transporter capacity ((transporter_friendly_flux))\n- Calcium arrives in small, manageable waves ((wave_pattern_absorption))\n- This prevents transporter overload and mineral waste ((controlled_bioavailability_profile))\n- The mineral–protein fragments interact well with intestinal surfaces ((intestinal_interface_affinity))\n\n### 🔄 ESC Enters the Bloodstream in a More Stable Form\n\nOnce absorbed, ESC continues to behave differently from rock calcium.\n- Protein associated calcium remains more stable in circulation ((circulatory_stability_support))\n- Less free calcium spikes, which the body tends to regulate away ((reduced_free_calcium_spike))\n- The mineral stays available for tissues that need it ((target_tissue_mineral_availability))\n- This supports smoother delivery to bones, joints, and cartilage ((systemic_mineral_delivery_efficiency))\n\n### 🦴 ESC Reaches Bone Building Sites More Effectively\n\nBecause ESC stays soluble and stable, more of it reaches the osteoblasts — the cells that build bone.\n- Calcium arrives in a form osteoblasts can readily incorporate ((osteoblast_ready_mineral_form))\n- The gradual release supports continuous mineralisation ((sustained_mineralisation_support))\n- Less calcium is lost to precipitation or excretion ((reduced_mineral_loss_pathways))\n- The body uses more of what you take — not just a fraction ((enhanced_utilisation_ratio))\n\n### 🟢 Outcome\n\nESC travels through the body in a guided, biologically aligned way: dissolving smoothly, staying soluble, moving with nutrient pathways, entering the bloodstream in a stable form, and reaching bone building cells efficiently.",
  "esc_calcium_comparison": "Calcium is one of the most essential minerals in human physiology, yet the body is selective about how it absorbs, transports, and uses it. The form of calcium you consume determines how smoothly it dissolves, how comfortably it moves through the digestive tract, how steadily it enters the bloodstream, and how efficiently it reaches bone building cells.\nCalcium carbonate behaves like ground stone.\nCalcium citrate behaves like a simple mineral salt.\nESC (Eggshell Calcium) behaves like a biological mineral — a form the body recognises, processes gently, and uses more efficiently.\nThis merged chapter follows the entire journey of these three calcium forms through the body, revealing why ESC consistently delivers superior comfort, stability, and utilisation.\n\n### 🫙 1. Dissolution — Where the Journey Begins\n\nDissolution is the first and most decisive step in calcium bioavailability. If a calcium form dissolves poorly, everything downstream — absorption, circulation, bone delivery — is compromised.\nESC (Eggshell Calcium)\n- Softens and dissolves gently, even at lower acidity ((gentle_dissolution_profile))\n- Breaks down evenly without gritty fragments\n- Avoids chalky heaviness and irritation\nCalcium Carbonate\n- Requires strong stomach acid to dissolve ((reduced_acid_dependency))\n- Dissolves slowly and inconsistently\n- Can trigger heaviness or reflux\nCalcium Citrate\n- Dissolves more easily than carbonate\n- Behaves like a salt, not a biological structure\n- Lacks ESC’s smooth, food like breakdown\n\n### 🚚 2. Digestive Comfort — How Each Form Feels in the Gut\n\nDigestive comfort is shaped by how lightly a mineral moves through the gut and how much residue it leaves behind.\nESC\n- Moves lightly through the digestive tract ((digestive_lightness_effect))\n- Leaves fewer dense residues\n- Minimises bloating, gas, and heaviness\nCalcium Carbonate\n- Leaves behind gritty mineral residues\n- Higher chance of bloating or constipation\n- Feels heavy for many users\nCalcium Citrate\n- Gentler than carbonate\n- Still produces salt like residues\n- Not as smooth or light as ESC\n\n### 🌊 3. Release Pattern — The Rhythm of Calcium Uptake\n\nThe body absorbs calcium best when it is released gradually, in a rhythm that matches transporter capacity.\nESC\n- Gradual, food like release ((stepwise_mineral_release))\n- Prevents transporter overload\n- Supports higher total uptake\nCalcium Carbonate\n- Releases calcium in a single burst\n- Overwhelms transporters\n- More calcium is wasted or excreted\nCalcium Citrate\n- Dissolves quickly and releases calcium rapidly\n- Short absorption window\n- Less efficient for sustained uptake\n\n### 💧 4. Solubility Stability — Staying Usable Through the Intestine\n\nOnce calcium leaves the stomach, the pH rises. Many calcium forms lose solubility here — but ESC does not.\nESC\n- Maintains solubility through its mineral–protein matrix ((matrix_assisted_solubility_support))\n- Stays available longer\n- Enables continuous absorption\nCalcium Carbonate\n- Solubility drops sharply after the stomach\n- Undissolved particles reduce usable calcium\n- Highly dependent on stomach acidity\nCalcium Citrate\n- More stable than carbonate\n- Still loses solubility as pH rises\n- Not protected by a biological matrix\n\n### 🔁 5. Circulation — How Calcium Enters and Moves Through the Bloodstream\n\nCalcium must enter the bloodstream in a stable form to remain available for tissues.\nESC\n- Stable bloodstream entry ((circulatory_stability_support))\n- Higher tissue availability ((target_tissue_mineral_availability))\n- Avoids sharp spikes of free calcium\nCalcium Carbonate\n- Produces free ion spikes\n- The body quickly regulates excess away\n- Less stable mineral availability\nCalcium Citrate\n- Similar free ion behaviour to carbonate\n- Shorter availability window\n- Lower tissue availability\n\n### 🦴 6. Bone Utilisation — The Final Destination\n\nUltimately, the goal of calcium supplementation is to support bone mineralisation.\nESC\n- Delivered in an osteoblast ready form ((osteoblast_ready_mineral_form))\n- Supports continuous mineralisation\n- Higher proportion of calcium becomes bone\nCalcium Carbonate\n- Must be reprocessed before use\n- More calcium lost to precipitation\n- Lower bone conversion\nCalcium Citrate\n- Moderate utilisation\n- Still not biologically aligned\n- Less efficient than ESC\n\n### 📊 Comparison Table — ESC vs Carbonate vs Citrate\n\n- Dissolution — ESC: Gentle, even, low acid; Carbonate: Requires strong acid, gritty; Citrate: Easy but salt like.\n- Digestive Comfort — ESC: Light, low residue; Carbonate: Heavy, constipating; Citrate: Moderate.\n- Release Pattern — ESC: Gradual waves; Carbonate: Single burst; Citrate: Rapid release.\n- Solubility Stability — ESC: High, matrix supported; Carbonate: Drops after stomach; Citrate: Moderate.\n- Circulation — ESC: Stable, protein associated; Carbonate: Free ion spikes; Citrate: Short window.\n- Bone Utilisation — ESC: Highest, osteoblast ready; Carbonate: Low efficiency; Citrate: Moderate."
};

